#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints a short story about a clockwork garden.

cat <<'EOF'
The Clockwork Garden

Behind the iron gate on Winding Street, time did not run forward. It wound. Brass vines climbed trellises that ticked once per minute; marigolds opened on cogs instead of sun, and the fountain in the center measured seasons by the splash of each drop against a copper bell.

Mira Vale tended the garden after her grandmother's keys passed to her—three keys for three seasons, none for winter, because winter was when the garden slept and remembered. Visitors came with broken watches and heavier hearts. Mira never asked why. She handed them shears trimmed with pearl and said, "Cut only what hums when you touch it. The rest is someone else's hour still running."

Old Tomas the horologist sat on the bench by the sundial that never cast a shadow. He had built the garden's heart—a mainspring wide as a wagon wheel—before anyone believed a machine could grow anything but rust. "Plants don't need sunlight here," he told every skeptic. "They need patience set to the right gear ratio."

One autumn—or the garden's imitation of autumn, when the leaves turned brass and chimed in the wind—a girl named Elin arrived carrying a seed in a locket. Her mother had died mid-sentence, leaving a word unfinished on the kitchen table. Elin could not bury the locket; she could not speak the word aloud. "Plant it," Mira said, though no soil in the garden had ever taken metal. They dug at the base of the whispering hedge. The seed did not sprout leaves. It sprang—a slender stem of filigree, at its tip a bud like a closed mouth.

Tomas adjusted the mainspring three teeth tighter. "Some grief is torque," he muttered. "Too much, and the whole mechanism strips."

The bud opened on the night the garden skipped its midnight chime. Inside lay not petals but a single syllable, etched in light, the last sound Elin's mother had almost made. Elin spoke it once. The hedge exhaled. Every cog in the garden turned one notch forward, together, and for one real second outside the gate, every stopped clock in the city jerked and ticked.

Mira wrote the syllable in the ledger she kept beside the fountain— not the letters, but the interval between breath and voice. Tomas oiled the wheel. Elin came back each week until her locket hung empty and her pockets held only ordinary seeds.

They say if you stand at the gate at dawn and listen, you can hear the garden counting: not toward doom, not toward noon, but toward the next small thing worth winding—a apology, a recipe, a name spoken aloud before the hour runs out.

The end.

EOF
